\subsection*{Problem 1}

\textbf{The correct answer to this problem is worth 10 points.}


\subsection*{Problem Statement}

There are 10 students in a class. Each of them reported how many pets they have. The numbers were as follows:

\[
 2, 0, 1, 3, 2, 1, 0, 4, 1, 1
\]

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Find the mean number of pets.
  \item Determine the mode.
  \item Find the median.
\end{enumerate}

\vspace{0.3cm}

\subsection*{Theory:}

\textbf{Mean} \\
The arithmetic mean is the result of dividing the sum of all numbers in a set by their total count.

\textit{Example in data analysis:} The average customer purchase amount. \\
\textit{Example in ML:} Mean Squared Error (MSE).

\vspace{0.3cm}

\textbf{Mode} \\
\textit{Formal definition:} value that appears most often in a set of data values \\
\\
\textit{Example in data analysis:} The most popular item in an online store. \\
\textit{Example in ML:} Choosing the final class by model voting.

\vspace{0.3cm}

\textbf{Median} \\
\textit{Simple explanation:} It's the middle value in an ordered list of numbers. \\
\textit{Formal definition:} The median divides a dataset in half — 50\% of the values are smaller, and 50\% are larger. \\
\textit{Example in data analysis:} Median salary. \\
\textit{Example in ML:} Median Absolute Error.

\vspace{0.3cm}

\subsection*{Answer Format:}
Output three numbers on a single line, separated by a space: first the mean, then the mode, and then the median.

\subsection*{Example for answer output:}
99 3.14 2.72

\end{problem}
